{{Short description|Parliamentary constituency in the United Kingdom, 2024 onwards}} {{Infobox UK constituency |name = Waveney Valley |parliament = uk |image = {{maplink|frame=yes|frame-align=center|plain=yes|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Waveney Valley (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame-height=200|frame-width=250}} |caption = Boundaries since 2024 |image2 = [[File:East of England - Waveney Valley constituency.svg|215px|alt=Map of constituency]] |caption2 = Boundary of Waveney Valley in the East of England |year = 2024 |abolished = |type = County |elects_howmany = One |next = |electorate = 70,540 (2023)<ref>{{cite web |url= https://boundarycommissionforengland.independent.gov.uk/2023-review/the-2023-review-of-parliamentary-constituency-boundaries-in-england-volume-two-constituency-names-designations-and-composition/the-2023-review-of-parliamentary-constituency-boundaries-in-england-volume-two-constituency-names-designations-and-composition-eastern/#lg_waveney-valley-cc-70540 |title= The 2023 Review of Parliamentary Constituency Boundaries in England – Volume two: Constituency names, designations and composition – Eastern |publisher=Boundary Commission for England |access-date=27 June 2024 |df=dmy }}</ref> |mp = [[Adrian Ramsay]] |party = [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green]] |region = England |county = [[Norfolk]] and [[Suffolk]] |towns = [[Bungay]], [[Diss, Norfolk|Diss]], [[Eye, Suffolk|Eye]], [[Halesworth]], [[Harleston, Norfolk|Harleston]] |previous = [[Waveney (UK Parliament constituency)|Waveney]]; [[Central Suffolk and North Ipswich]]; [[Bury St Edmunds (UK Parliament constituency)|Bury St. Edmunds]]; [[Suffolk Coastal (UK Parliament constituency)| Suffolk Coastal]] & [[South Norfolk (UK Parliament constituency)|South Norfolk]] }}
'''Waveney Valley''' is a [[List of UK Parliament constituencies|constituency]] of the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] in the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=The 2023 Review of Parliamentary Constituency Boundaries in England – Volume one: Report – Eastern {{!}} Boundary Commission for England |url=https://boundarycommissionforengland.independent.gov.uk/2023-review/2023-review-volume-one-report/the-2023-review-of-parliamentary-constituency-boundaries-in-england-volume-one-report-eastern/ |access-date=2023-07-14 |website=boundarycommissionforengland.independent.gov.uk}}</ref> Since [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024]], it has been represented by [[Adrian Ramsay]] of the [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green Party.]]
== Boundaries == The constituency straddles the [[River Waveney]] between [[Norfolk]] and [[Suffolk]] and is composed of the following (as they existed on 1 December 2020):
* The District of East Suffolk wards of: Bungay & Wainford; Halesworth & Blything.
* The District of Mid Suffolk wards of: Bacton; Eye; Fressingfield; Gislingham; Haughley, Stowupland & Wetherden; Hoxne & Worlingworth; Mendlesham; Palgrave; Rickinghall; Stradbroke & Laxfield; Walsham-le-Willows.
* The District of South Norfolk wards of: Beck Vale, Dickleburgh & Scole; Bressingham & Burston; Bunwell; Diss & Roydon; Ditchingham & Earsham; Harleston.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made |at=Schedule I Part 2 Eastern region}}</ref>
It covers the following areas: * Areas to the north of the river, including [[Diss, Norfolk|Diss]] and [[Harleston, Norfolk|Harleston]], transferred from [[South Norfolk (UK Parliament constituency)|South Norfolk]] * [[Bungay]], transferred from [[Waveney (UK Parliament constituency)|Waveney]] (to be renamed [[Lowestoft (UK Parliament constituency)|Lowestoft]]) * [[Halesworth]], transferred from [[Suffolk Coastal (UK Parliament constituency)|Suffolk Coastal]] * Rural areas of north Suffolk, including [[Eye, Suffolk|Eye]], transferred from [[Central Suffolk and North Ipswich (UK Parliament constituency)|Central Suffolk and North Ipswich]], and [[Bury St Edmunds (UK Parliament constituency)|Bury St Edmunds]] (to be renamed [[Bury St Edmunds and Stowmarket (UK Parliament constituency)|Bury St Edmunds and Stowmarket]])
==Constituency profile== [[Electoral Calculus]] characterises the proposed seat as "Strong Right", with right-wing economic and social views, high home ownership levels and strong support for Brexit.<ref>{{Cite web |title=New Seat Details - Waveney Valley|url=https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/fcgi-bin/calcwork23.py?seat=Waveney+Valley |access-date=2024-02-06 |website=www.electoralcalculus.co.uk}}</ref>
The seat is a target seat for the [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green Party]]<ref>{{Cite news |last=Walker |first=Peter |last2= |first2= |date=2023-10-06 |title=Green party says it plans to focus its effort on four seats at general election |url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2023/oct/06/green-party-conference-accuse-labour-bystander-conservative-chaos |access-date=2024-02-06 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> who won half of the council wards in the seat [[2023 United Kingdom local elections|in the 2023]] local elections.
