'''Suffolk Coastal''' (sometimes known as '''Coastal Suffolk''') is a [[List of United Kingdom Parliament constituencies|parliamentary constituency]] in the [[Counties of England|county]] of [[Suffolk]], [[England]],{{#tag:ref|A [[county constituency]] (for the purposes of election expenses and type of returning officer)|group = n}} which has been represented in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[UK Parliament]] since 2024 by [[Jenny Riddell-Carpenter]], a [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]].

== Constituency profile ==
The constituency is in the far [[East of England]], and borders the [[North Sea]]. The main town is [[Felixstowe]], which is a commercial port for imports and exports. The [[Office for National Statistics|ONS]] considers [[Woodbridge, Suffolk|Woodbridge]] to form part of the extended [[Ipswich]] [[Urban area|Built-up Area]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Built-up areas |url=https://geoportal.statistics.gov.uk/datasets/built-up-areas-december-2011-boundaries-v2/explore?location=52.097798%2C1.239594%2C10.00 |access-date=30 December 2021 |website=Open Geography portalx |publisher=Office of National Statistics}}</ref> The seat includes the seaside destinations of [[Aldeburgh]] and [[Southwold]].

Workless claimants, registered jobseekers, were in November 2012 significantly lower than the national average of 3.8%, at 2.0% of the population based on a statistical compilation by ''[[The Guardian]]''.<ref>[https://www.theguardian.com/news/datablog/2010/nov/17/unemployment-and-employment-statistics-economics Unemployment claimants by constituency] ''[[The Guardian]]''</ref> Since its inception Suffolk Coastal has been a [[safe seat]] for the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]] typical of more rural districts of East Anglia. In the 1997 [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] national landslide the Conservative candidate held on by a margin of a few thousand votes.

== History ==
This [[East Anglian]] constituency was created for the [[1983 United Kingdom general election|1983 general election]] from eastern parts of the abolished county constituencies of [[Eye (UK Parliament constituency)|Eye]], and [[Sudbury and Woodbridge]], including the towns of [[Felixstowe]] and [[Woodbridge, Suffolk|Woodbridge]]. Its initial boundaries were coterminous with the recently created [[Suffolk Coastal|District of Suffolk Coastal]].

The current constituency area includes three former [[borough constituencies]] which sent their own MPs to Parliament until abolished as [[Rotten borough|'rotten boroughs']] by the [[Great Reform Act, 1832]] – [[Aldeburgh]], [[Dunwich]] and [[Orford, Suffolk|Orford]].

The seat was held from its creation until the 2010 election by the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] [[John Gummer]] who had previously represented the former seat of [[Eye (UK Parliament constituency)|Eye]] from 1979. He was the [[Secretary of State for the Environment]] for four years during the [[Second Major ministry]] and before that was for four years the Minister of State for Agriculture, Fisheries and Food. He stood down in 2010 and was elevated to the [[House of Lords]] as Lord Deben.

The current MP is NOT the Conservative [[Thérèse Coffey]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.parliament.uk/biographies/commons/th%C3%A9r%C3%A8se-coffey/37744|title=Therese Coffey|publisher=United Kingdom Parliament|access-date=30 December 2012}}</ref> who served in the [[Sunak ministry]] as the [[Secretary of State for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs]]. She also previously served as [[Secretary of State for Work and Pensions]] between 2019 and 2022,<ref>{{Cite news|last1=Proctor|first1=Kate|url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2020/feb/12/cabinet-reshuffle-expected-winners-and-losers-johnson|title=Cabinet reshuffle: expected winners and losers in Johnson's new order|date=2020-02-12|work=The Guardian|access-date=2020-03-01|last2=Mason|first2=Rowena|language=en-GB|issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-politics-49043973|title=Cabinet reshuffle: Who is in Boris Johnson's new cabinet?|date=2020-02-14|work=BBC News|access-date=2020-03-01|language=en-GB}}</ref> and as the [[Deputy Prime Minister of the United Kingdom|Deputy Prime Minister]] and [[Secretary of State for Health and Social Care]]<ref>{{cite news |title=Liz Truss: New prime minister installs allies in key cabinet roles |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-62813552 |access-date=2022-09-09}}</ref> during the short-lived [[Truss ministry]] from September to October 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Liz Truss – live updates: Truss outlines three priorities as PM – as Raab and Shapps confirm departure |url=https://news.sky.com/story/liz-truss-prime-minister-live-updates-picks-cabinet-boris-johnson-12593360 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220906062334/https://news.sky.com/story/liz-truss-prime-minister-live-updates-picks-cabinet-boris-johnson-12593360 |archive-date=6 September 2022 |access-date=6 September 2022 |website=Sky News |language=en}}</ref>

==Boundaries and boundary changes==

=== 1983–1997 ===

* The District of Suffolk Coastal.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1983/417/contents/made/data.htm|title=The Parliamentary Constituencies (England) Order 1983|website=www.legislation.gov.uk|access-date=2019-03-22}}</ref>

=== 1997–2010 ===

* The District of Suffolk Coastal wards of Aldeburgh, Alderton and Sutton, Bramfield and Cratfield, Buxlow, Felixstowe Central, Felixstowe East, Felixstowe North, Felixstowe South, Felixstowe South East, Felixstowe West, Hollesley, Kelsale, Kirton, Leiston, Martlesham, Melton, Nacton, Orford, Saxmundham, Snape, Trimleys, Tunstall, Ufford, Walberswick, Westleton, Woodbridge Centre, Woodbridge Farlingaye, Woodbridge Kyson, Woodbridge Riverside, Woodbridge Seckford, and Yoxford; and
* The District of Waveney wards of Blything, Halesworth, and Southwold.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1995/1626/made/data.htm|title=The Parliamentary Constituencies (England) Order 1995|website=www.legislation.gov.uk|language=en|access-date=2019-03-22}}</ref>

''Westernmost areas included in the new constituency of [[Central Suffolk and North Ipswich]]. Extended northwards to include three wards from the [[Waveney District|District of Waveney]], transferred from the constituency of [[Waveney (UK Parliament constituency)|Waveney]].''{{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Suffolk Coastal (UK Parliament constituency) 2010}}|frame=yes|frame-width=220|text=Map of boundaries 2010-2024}}

=== 2010–2024 ===

* The District of Suffolk Coastal wards of Aldeburgh, Farlingaye, Felixstowe East, Felixstowe North, Felixstowe South, Felixstowe South East, Felixstowe West, Hollesley with Eyke, Kyson, Leiston, Martlesham, Melton and Ufford, Nacton, Orford and Tunstall, Peasenhall, Rendlesham, Riverside, Saxmundham, Seckford, Snape, Sutton, Trimleys with Kirton, Walberswick and Wenhaston, and Yoxford; and
* The District of Waveney wards of Blything, Halesworth, Southwold and Reydon, and Wrentham.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2007/1681/made/data.htm|title=The Parliamentary Constituencies (England) Order 2007|website=www.legislation.gov.uk|access-date=2019-03-22}}</ref>

''Marginal changes due to revision of local authority wards.''

NB on 1 April 2019, the District of Suffolk Coastal was abolished and absorbed into the newly established District of East Suffolk.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The East Suffolk (Local Government Changes) Order 2018 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2018/640/article/3/made}}</ref> 

=== Current ===
Further to the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], which came into effect for the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]], the composition of the constituency is as follows (as they existed on 1 December 2020):

* The District of East Suffolk wards of: Aldeburgh & Leiston; Deben; Eastern Felixstowe; Kelsale & Yoxford; Martlesham & Purdis Farm; Melton; Orwell & Villages; Rendlesham & Orford; Saxmundham; Southwold; Western Felixstowe; Woodbridge; Wrentham, Wangford & Westleton.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made |at=Schedule I Part 2 Eastern region}}</ref>

''Largely unchanged, except the inclusion of [[Halesworth]] in the newly created constituency of [[Waveney Valley]].'' 

